From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:33:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] An image that the application reads can be read in chunks,
 the last ones kept

A disc image that the application reads itself (bd_open_stream()) is read
block by block, as libudfread and the reader ask for them. Opening a disc
reads many small pieces of it, here and there: the descriptors of the
volume, directories and file entries, the playlists and the clip
information of each title. Over a network each read that does not follow
the one before is a new request, with its round trip. Kodi plays an image
that it opens directly in this mode, and reads it without its cache, so
an image over HTTP (a .strm file that names one, an image on a cloud
drive) cost a request for most of those pieces.

BLURAY_PLAYER_SETTING_IMAGE_CACHE (0x1103, set before the disc is opened)
is the KiB of chunks kept: the image is then read in aligned chunks of
64 KiB, and the chunks read last are kept. Most of those pieces then come
from a chunk already read, and the clips are read a chunk at a time. A
read gives what it gave without the cache: a chunk that a read gives only
in part is asked for again from where the read stopped, and whatever the
cache cannot give (the end of the image, a block that cannot be read) is
read as asked. Off (0) by default: nothing changes until the application
sets it.

Measured with an image of 121 playlists and 61 clips (the main title is
the Dolby Vision FEL test stream) opened as Kodi opens one (open, the
titles, the information of each, the longest title, 16 MiB of it, a seek
to its middle and one to a quarter with 4 MiB after each), and the reads
counted through a model of Kodi's HTTP reader (CCurlFile: a read that
starts within the 96 KiB behind its position, or less than 32 KiB ahead
of it, needs no new request): 5380 reads and 259 requests before, 407
reads and 8 requests with 4096 KiB; the same bytes, the same titles and
the same longest title. Reads that give at most 5 blocks: the same blocks
read and the same requests. A block that cannot be read: the same bytes as
without the cache. Off and at 0, the same reads in the same order as
before. The tests of the library pass (59 checks).
